## Architecture - How the single RMAP.lib is assembled

Each module is a CMake **OBJECT** library (defined in its own `CMakeLists.txt`). The
top-level `CMakeLists.txt` creates one `add_library(RMAP STATIC ...)` target and pulls in
each enabled module's compiled objects via generator expressions:

```cmake
add_library(RMAP STATIC $<TARGET_OBJECTS:RMAP_Objects>)
# ... then, guarded by each option:
target_sources(RMAP PRIVATE $<TARGET_OBJECTS:RMAP_Svc_SB_Objects>)
target_include_directories(RMAP PUBLIC $<BUILD_INTERFACE:.../RMAP_Svc_SB/include>)
```

Public include dirs are declared PUBLIC on the final `RMAP` target: per-module
`$<BUILD_INTERFACE:.../include>` while building, plus a single `$<INSTALL_INTERFACE:include>`
for the installed/flattened layout. There is also an alias `RMAP::RMAP`.

Within each module's own `CMakeLists.txt`: the OBJECT library sets `cxx_std_17`, a PUBLIC
`$<BUILD_INTERFACE:.../include>`, and (for services only) a PRIVATE include of
`$<TARGET_PROPERTY:RMAP_Objects,INTERFACE_INCLUDE_DIRECTORIES>` so service TUs can see
core headers at compile time.

### KEY DECISION - assembly via `$<TARGET_OBJECTS>`, NOT `target_link_libraries`

The approved plan's snippet showed `target_link_libraries(RMAP PUBLIC RMAP_Objects)` to
merge the object libraries. During implementation this was changed to
`$<TARGET_OBJECTS:...>` (objects added as sources) plus explicit
`target_include_directories` on `RMAP`. Rationale (important - do not "fix" this back):

- `target_link_libraries(RMAP PUBLIC <objlib>)` records the object library as a usage
  requirement in `RMAP`'s `INTERFACE_LINK_LIBRARIES`. That forces every object library
  into the `install(EXPORT RMAPTargets)` set (CMake errors: target not in any export set),
  and would otherwise install stray intermediate artifacts.
- `$<TARGET_OBJECTS:...>` bakes the compiled objects directly into the archive with **no**
  link dependency. The export set is therefore cleanly just the `RMAP` target, and each
  module's objects appear exactly once (no duplicate symbols).
- Because core is always built, `RMAP` always has at least core's objects, so CMake
  resolves the STATIC target's linker language without needing a dummy source file.

### Install / find_package

`install(TARGETS RMAP EXPORT RMAPTargets ARCHIVE DESTINATION lib)`, per-module
`install(DIRECTORY <Module>/include/ DESTINATION include)` (guarded by each option),
`install(EXPORT RMAPTargets NAMESPACE RMAP:: ...)`, and
`configure_package_config_file` + `write_basic_package_version_file` using
`cmake/RMAPConfig.cmake.in` so downstream projects can `find_package(RMAP)` and link
`RMAP::RMAP`. Package config installs to `lib/cmake/RMAP`.

## Build & Verify

Toolchain confirmed on this machine: CMake 3.30.3; Visual Studio 17 2022 / MSVC
19.44 (auto-detected by CMake's default VS generator). MSVC `cl` is NOT on the plain
PowerShell PATH, but the VS generator locates it, so a VS Developer prompt is not
required for the default generator.

```powershell
# Default (all services)
cmake -S . -B build
cmake --build build --config Release          # -> build\Release\RMAP.lib

# Exclude services (example)
cmake -S . -B build_excl -DRMAP_BUILD_SVC_REST=OFF -DRMAP_BUILD_SVC_SOCKETIO=OFF
cmake --build build_excl --config Release      # -> build_excl\Release\RMAP.lib (core + SB only)
```

Inspect symbols with dumpbin (path may vary by MSVC version):
`& "<...>\VC\Tools\MSVC\14.44.35207\bin\Hostx64\x64\dumpbin.exe" /linkermember:1 build\Release\RMAP.lib`

### Verification results (2026-07-23)
- Default build produced exactly one deliverable, `build\Release\RMAP.lib`, containing
  all four modules' symbols: `?GetName@RMAP@@`, `?GetVersion@RMAP@@`,
  `?Svc_SB_GetName@RMAP@@`, `?Svc_Rest_GetName@RMAP@@`, `?Svc_SocketIO_GetName@RMAP@@`.
- Exclusion build (`-DRMAP_BUILD_SVC_REST=OFF -DRMAP_BUILD_SVC_SOCKETIO=OFF`) produced
  one `RMAP.lib` with only core + `Svc_SB`; REST and SocketIO symbols were absent.
- Note on intermediate `.lib` files: under `build\<module>\<module>.dir\Release\` MSVC
  emits a per-OBJECT-library `.lib` (e.g. `RMAP_Objects.lib`). These are MSVC build
  byproducts, NOT deliverables and NOT installed. The only deliverable is
  `build\<config>\RMAP.lib`.
- Both throwaway build directories were deleted after verification; only source remains.

## Conventions

All Metaversal standards apply (see global `~/.cursor/rules/*.mdc`): 3-space indent,
Allman braces, Hungarian notation, single return, ALL-CAPS types/constants, Capital-first
function names, no tabs, no Python, symmetry (Rule 1). The four modules are structured
symmetrically: the three service `CMakeLists.txt` files are line-for-line identical except
the module name; the core `CMakeLists.txt` differs only by omitting the core-header
propagation line (it does not need to include itself).

Target naming: the final STATIC target is `RMAP` (produces `RMAP.lib`); the OBJECT
targets are `<Module>_Objects` (chosen so the core object target `RMAP_Objects` does not
collide with the final `RMAP` target name). CMake options are `RMAP_BUILD_SVC_<NAME>`.

